{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"The progression E–A–B is most likely in the key of E major, so you can transpose it down a whole step to D major, giving D–G–A. In E major the three chords are all diatonic (E major, A major, B major) – they are the I, IV, and V chords of that key, which is why the top‑match list shows E major with three of the four chords fitting. To sing lower you move every chord down the same interval (a whole‑step):\r\n\r\n- **D major** (D F♯ A) → replaces E major\r\n- **G major** (G B D) → replaces A major\r\n- **A major** (A C♯ E) → replaces B major\r\n\r\nIn the new key of D major these are again I, IV, V. The natural D‑major scale is: D E F♯ G A B C♯. Listening for the tonic (the chord that feels like “home”) will confirm the key – in the original you’ll hear a resolution to E major, and after transposition the resolution will be to D major. This keeps the same functional feel while lowering the vocal range.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4219,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":4,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-1","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"song is E A B but i cant sing that high, need it in D","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":4}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"Yes, the chords E major (E‑G♯‑B) and C major (C‑E‑G) share the note **E**.  \r\n\r\nIn the key of **C major**, C major is the I‑chord (C‑E‑G). The E‑major chord (E‑G♯‑B) functions as the V/vi (a secondary dominant leading to the vi‑chord A minor), and its only common tone with the I‑chord is the root‑position note E.  \r\n\r\nIf we look at the **E minor** triad (E‑G‑B), which is the iii‑chord in C major, it shares **E** and **G** with C major, giving two common tones.  \r\n\r\nSo, depending on the quality of the E‑chord, they share one note (E) for E major, or two notes (E and G) for E minor, while both are examined within the C major key context.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2909,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":5,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-12","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"do E and C have any notes in common at all","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":5}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The distance from G up to E is called a **major sixth**.\r\n\r\nA major sixth spans eight whole‑step intervals (9 semitones). Counting diatonically from G: G‑A‑B‑C‑D‑E gives six letter names, and the interval’s quality is determined by the number of semitones (9), which matches the definition of a major sixth.\r\n\r\nBelow are common chords that contain this interval, shown with their constituent notes. I’ll work in the key of **C major**, where G is the dominant (V) and E is the mediant (III).\r\n\r\n- **G major** (G B D) – the root‑third‑fifth of the V chord.  \r\n- **E minor** (E G B) – the iii chord, built on the note E.  \r\n- **G6** (G B D E) – a G major triad with the added major sixth (E).  \r\n- **C major** (C E G) – the I chord, containing the interval E up to G (the reverse direction).\r\n\r\n**Playable shapes**\r\n\r\nG6 (open position) – adds the high‑E string open for the sixth:\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3.0/2.1)\r\n```\r\n\r\nE minor (open position) – the basic Em shape:\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/6.2/5.2/4.0/3.0/2.1)\r\n```","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4454,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":2,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-20","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"from G up to E what do you call that distance","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"In D major the diatonic chords are D (D‑F♯‑A), Em (E‑G‑B), F♯m (F♯‑A‑C♯), G (G‑B‑D), A (A‑C♯‑E), Bm (B‑D‑F♯), and C♯dim (C♯‑E‑G).  \r\n\r\nThese seven triads come from stacking thirds on each scale degree of the D‑major scale (D E F♯ G A B C♯). D is the I chord (tonic), Em the ii (minor subdominant), F♯m the iii (minor mediant), G the IV (subdominant), A the V (dominant), Bm the vi (relative minor), and C♯dim the vii° (leading‑tone chord).  \r\n\r\nIn practice you’ll often see these chords extended to sevenths: Dmaj7 (D‑F♯‑A‑C♯), Em7 (E‑G‑B‑D), F♯m7 (F♯‑A‑C♯‑E), Gmaj7 (G‑B‑D‑F♯), A7 (A‑C♯‑E‑G), Bm7 (B‑D‑F♯‑A), and C♯m7♭5 (C♯‑E‑G‑B).  \r\n\r\nBeyond the diatonic pool, common functional additions include the secondary dominant E7 (V/V, notes E‑G♯‑B‑D) that resolves to A, and the borrowed bVII G (♭VII, notes G‑B‑D) for a mixolydian flavor, both of which fit naturally in a D‑major context.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2479,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":18,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-28","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"chords that work in D major?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["Dmaj7 (D-F#-A-C#)","Gmaj7 (G-B-D-F#)","A7 (A-C#-E-G)"],"verdict":"F","verified":7}
