{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"Transposed up a whole step the progression becomes Bm (B D F#), G (G B D), D (D F# A), A (A C# E). The original set of chords Am‑F‑C‑G fits perfectly in both C major and its relative minor A minor, because each of those keys contains all four chords diatonically (C major diatonic set: C, Dm, Em, F, G, Am, Bdim; A minor diatonic set: Am, Bdim, C, Dm, Em, F, G). To decide which key the song is really in you listen for the tonal centre: if the music resolves to C (the I chord) or sounds ‘home’ on C, it’s in C major; if it settles on Am (the i chord) or the melody centers on A, it’s in A minor. In the more common major interpretation (C major), the Roman‑numeral analysis is vi‑IV‑I‑V, so after moving everything up a whole step into D major the functions become vi‑IV‑I‑V as Bm‑G‑D‑A. The D major scale is D E F# G A B C#, which provides the notes for each of those chords. If you prefer the minor view (A minor), the same transposition lands you in B minor, where the same functions apply (vi‑IV‑I‑V become Bm‑G‑D‑A) using the B natural minor scale B C# D E F# G A.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4084,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":5,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-73","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"put Am F C G up a whole step pls","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":5}
